::ENG1_U02_L02_Q01::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: At first, Sana refused to ask for help with the mural. After spilling paint across the sketch, she let two classmates repair the lines with her. The finished wall held all three signatures. Question: Which theme is best supported?{
~A. Art is only successful when it is perfect from the start.#Incorrect. The mural improves after a mistake.
~B. Classmates should never sign shared work.#Incorrect. The signatures show shared effort.
=C. Collaboration can improve work that one person cannot finish alone.#Correct. Sana accepts help, and the final mural includes all three students.
~D. Paint spills always ruin a project permanently.#Incorrect. The project is repaired.
}

::ENG1_U02_L02_Q02::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: Nolan laughed when his little brother packed a flashlight for the short walk. When the streetlights went out, Nolan followed the small beam all the way home. Question: Which theme develops?{
=A. Wisdom can come from someone others underestimate.#Correct. Nolan dismisses his brother, but the flashlight becomes useful.
~B. Younger siblings are always wrong.#Incorrect. The younger brother's choice helps.
~C. Flashlights cause streetlights to fail.#Incorrect. The flashlight does not cause the outage.
~D. Walking is better than driving.#Incorrect. Transportation comparison is not the theme.
}

::ENG1_U02_L02_Q03::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: The old tree fell during the storm, and Mira cried over the empty space. Weeks later, she planted three saplings where the roots had been. Question: Which theme is strongest?{
~A. Storms happen only in forests.#Incorrect. The setting is not limited that way.
~B. Trees should never be replaced.#Incorrect. Mira plants saplings.
~C. Crying solves every problem.#Incorrect. Mira also takes action.
=D. Loss can lead to renewal when people choose to rebuild.#Correct. Mira responds to loss by planting new trees.
}

::ENG1_U02_L02_Q04::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: At the start, Leo kept score during every family game. By the end, he stopped counting points and taught his cousin how to play. Question: How does Leo's change develop theme?{
~A. It shows that games have no rules.#Incorrect. Teaching the cousin still involves rules.
=B. It suggests that sharing joy can matter more than winning.#Correct. Leo moves from scorekeeping to helping someone else enjoy the game.
~C. It proves Leo dislikes winning forever.#Incorrect. The passage shows a shift in values, not permanent dislike.
~D. It shows that cousins cannot learn games.#Incorrect. Leo teaches the cousin.
}

::ENG1_U02_L02_Q05::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: Amara hid the cracked bowl behind the others. Her grandmother placed it in the center of the table and said the gold repair made it stronger. Question: Which theme is best supported?{
~A. Bowls should never be used at dinner.#Incorrect. The bowl is placed on the table.
=B. Imperfections can become part of something's value.#Correct. The repaired crack is treated as strength, not shame.
~C. Grandmothers dislike broken objects.#Incorrect. The grandmother values the repaired bowl.
~D. Gold is always easy to find.#Incorrect. The source of gold is not the meaning.
}

::ENG1_U02_L02_Q06::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: Ben wanted the solo, but when his friend's voice shook during rehearsal, Ben sang the harmony softly until she found the melody. Question: Which theme develops through Ben's action?{
~A. Harmony is louder than melody.#Incorrect. The passage says Ben sings softly.
~B. Solos are always unfair.#Incorrect. The passage does not judge solos as unfair.
~C. Rehearsal should be silent.#Incorrect. Singing happens during rehearsal.
=D. Supporting someone else can be more meaningful than standing out alone.#Correct. Ben helps his friend instead of focusing only on the solo.
}

::ENG1_U02_L02_Q07::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: The first time Lina entered the library, she whispered that the books looked too difficult. By winter, she was leaving notes in the margins and recommending titles to her brother. Question: Which theme is developed?{
=A. Confidence can grow through repeated effort.#Correct. Lina moves from intimidation to active reading and recommending.
~B. Libraries are only for winter.#Incorrect. Winter marks time, not the message.
~C. Brothers cannot choose books.#Incorrect. The brother is only mentioned as someone receiving recommendations.
~D. Margins should stay empty.#Incorrect. The margin notes show engagement.
}

::ENG1_U02_L02_Q08::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: The village well ran dry, so each family carried one small jar of water to the oldest neighbor's door. Question: Which theme is best supported?{
~A. Wells never help communities.#Incorrect. The issue is the dry well, not all wells.
~B. Old neighbors should live alone.#Incorrect. The families support the neighbor.
=C. Community care matters most during shared hardship.#Correct. Each family gives a little during a difficult time.
~D. Small jars are better than large jars.#Incorrect. Jar size is not the lesson.
}

::ENG1_U02_L02_Q09::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: At first, Carter blamed the wind for every missed shot. Later, he adjusted his stance and practiced until the ball finally reached the goal. Question: Which theme develops?{
~A. Wind is always responsible for failure.#Incorrect. Carter learns to adjust his own stance.
~B. Soccer goals are easy to reach.#Incorrect. The passage shows practice and difficulty.
~C. Practice makes people avoid responsibility.#Incorrect. Practice shows responsibility here.
=D. Growth begins when people stop blaming and start adjusting.#Correct. Carter moves from excuses to action.
}

::ENG1_U02_L02_Q10::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: Priya kept the recipe secret until the festival oven broke. Then she taught everyone the steps so the food could still be served. Question: How does the conflict develop theme?{
=A. It shows that sharing knowledge can help a community solve a problem.#Correct. The broken oven leads Priya to teach others.
~B. It shows that ovens are never needed.#Incorrect. The broken oven creates a problem but does not remove the need for cooking.
~C. It proves recipes should be hidden forever.#Incorrect. Priya shares the recipe.
~D. It shows festivals are only about food.#Incorrect. The theme is broader than food.
}

::ENG1_U02_L02_Q11::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: Mason carried the heaviest box because he wanted praise. When no one noticed, he still returned for the last box after seeing his tired neighbor. Question: Which theme is best supported?{
~A. Boxes should always be heavy.#Incorrect. The box is part of the situation, not the message.
=B. True helpfulness can grow beyond wanting recognition.#Correct. Mason begins wanting praise but continues because someone needs help.
~C. Neighbors never notice anything.#Incorrect. The passage does not make that broad claim.
~D. Moving boxes is impossible.#Incorrect. Mason carries them.
}

::ENG1_U02_L02_Q12::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: The cracked sidewalk made the race uneven, so Tessa slowed down to run beside a younger student instead of sprinting ahead. Question: Which theme develops through Tessa's choice?{
~A. Sidewalks should be replaced immediately.#Incorrect. That is a possible issue but not the theme.
~B. Running slowly always wins races.#Incorrect. Winning is not the focus.
=C. Kindness may require giving up an advantage.#Correct. Tessa chooses to help rather than sprint ahead.
~D. Younger students cannot run.#Incorrect. The passage does not say that.
}

::ENG1_U02_L02_Q13::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: The narrator begins by calling the stray cat a nuisance. After feeding it through three cold nights, she calls it "the small heartbeat on the porch." Question: What theme is developing?{
~A. Cats cannot survive winter.#Incorrect. The passage does not state that.
=B. Care can change how people see another living thing.#Correct. The narrator's view changes through repeated care.
~C. Porches are warmer than houses.#Incorrect. That is not supported.
~D. Narrators should dislike animals.#Incorrect. The narrator's attitude changes.
}

::ENG1_U02_L02_Q14::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: At first, the family packed only what was new. Before leaving, they added the scratched table where every birthday candle had burned. Question: Which theme is best supported?{
=A. Memories can give ordinary objects deep value.#Correct. The table matters because of family memories.
~B. New things are always more valuable than old things.#Incorrect. The family chooses the old table.
~C. Tables are difficult to move.#Incorrect. Moving difficulty is not the focus.
~D. Birthday candles damage every home.#Incorrect. That is too literal and unsupported.
}

::ENG1_U02_L02_Q15::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: Hugo deleted his first paragraph five times. When he finally left one imperfect sentence on the screen, the rest of the essay began to follow. Question: Which theme is strongest?{
~A. Computers write essays for students.#Incorrect. Hugo is doing the writing.
~B. Deleting work always solves writing problems.#Incorrect. Progress begins when he stops deleting.
~C. Essays should never be revised.#Incorrect. The passage does not reject revision.
=D. Beginning imperfectly can be the first step toward progress.#Correct. Leaving one imperfect sentence allows him to continue.
}

::ENG1_U02_L02_Q16::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: After the power outage, the family complained about the dark. Then Grandma told stories until everyone leaned closer, listening. Question: How does the setting develop theme?{
=A. It shows that difficulty can create unexpected connection.#Correct. The outage creates a shared storytelling moment.
~B. It shows electricity is unimportant forever.#Incorrect. That is too broad because the passage focuses on one moment of storytelling during an outage.
~C. It proves darkness is always enjoyable.#Incorrect. The family first complains.
~D. It shows stories require no audience.#Incorrect. Everyone listens, so the audience is part of how the moment develops.
}

::ENG1_U02_L02_Q17::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: Sofia wanted to erase the old team record from the board. Instead, she wrote the new record beneath it so both names could be seen. Question: Which theme develops?{
~A. Records should never change.#Incorrect. Sofia adds the new record.
~B. Boards are hard to write on.#Incorrect. Writing difficulty is not the point.
=C. Honoring others can be part of personal success.#Correct. Sofia keeps the old record visible while adding her achievement.
~D. Teams should avoid success.#Incorrect. Success is recognized.
}

::ENG1_U02_L02_Q18::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: At first, Andre refused to read the letter because the handwriting looked unfamiliar. By the end, he traced each line slowly and smiled at his grandfather's signature. Question: What theme is developed?{
~A. Handwriting is never meaningful.#Incorrect. The handwriting becomes important.
=B. Understanding may require patience with what first feels unfamiliar.#Correct. Andre moves from refusal to careful reading.
~C. Letters should not be read slowly.#Incorrect. Slow reading shows care.
~D. Grandfathers never write letters.#Incorrect. The grandfather wrote this one.
}

::ENG1_U02_L02_Q19::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: The first snow ruined the outdoor concert. The musicians moved under the bus shelter and played until strangers began humming along. Question: Which theme is strongest?{
~A. Snow always ruins music permanently.#Incorrect. Music continues, so the passage does not support the idea that snow permanently destroys it.
~B. Bus shelters are concert halls.#Incorrect. That is too literal.
~C. Strangers never enjoy music.#Incorrect. They hum along, which shows they are affected by or joining the music.
=D. Creativity can turn an obstacle into a shared experience.#Correct. The musicians adapt and bring strangers together.
}

::ENG1_U02_L02_Q20::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: Emma hid the torn costume in her bag. When her friend admitted being afraid to perform, Emma pulled out the costume and said, "Then we'll be brave and messy together." Question: Which element develops the theme?{
~A. The bag's color#Incorrect. The color is not mentioned or important.
~B. The exact size of the stage#Incorrect. Stage size is not given.
=C. Emma's choice to reveal imperfection and support her friend#Correct. Her action develops a theme about shared courage.
~D. A weather detail#Incorrect. No weather detail appears.
}

::ENG1_U02_L02_Q21::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: The younger players missed every pass in the first half. In the second half, the older players slowed their throws and called each name before passing. Question: Which theme develops?{
=A. Good teamwork adjusts so everyone can participate.#Correct. The older players change their actions to include the younger players.
~B. Younger players should never play games.#Incorrect. The passage shows support, not exclusion.
~C. Passing is impossible to learn.#Incorrect. The players adjust.
~D. Names are more important than practice.#Incorrect. Calling names is one part of teamwork.
}

::ENG1_U02_L02_Q22::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: The narrator first describes the apartment as "too small for our dreams." Later, after neighbors gather there to plan a fundraiser, the narrator calls it "the room where our dreams learned to speak." Question: What theme develops?{
~A. Small rooms cannot hold people.#Incorrect. Neighbors gather there.
~B. Fundraisers always need large buildings.#Incorrect. This fundraiser planning happens in the apartment.
~C. Dreams are impossible to discuss.#Incorrect. The ending suggests dreams are spoken.
=D. A place can gain meaning through shared purpose.#Correct. The apartment's meaning changes through community action.
}

::ENG1_U02_L02_Q23::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: Marta kept the apology in her notebook for three days. On the fourth day, she read it aloud even though her voice shook. Question: Which theme is supported?{
~A. Notebooks should stay closed.#Incorrect. The notebook is not the message.
=B. Courage can mean speaking honestly even while afraid.#Correct. Marta reads the apology despite shaking.
~C. Apologies should never be written.#Incorrect. The written apology helps Marta prepare.
~D. Voices should never shake.#Incorrect. Her shaking shows fear, not failure.
}

::ENG1_U02_L02_Q24::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: The class plant wilted because everyone thought someone else had watered it. After that, the students made a shared schedule and the plant grew new leaves. Question: Which theme develops?{
~A. Plants do not need water.#Incorrect. Lack of water causes the problem.
~B. Schedules are useless.#Incorrect. The schedule helps.
=C. Shared responsibility works better than assuming someone else will act.#Correct. The students solve the problem by taking organized responsibility.
~D. Classrooms should not have plants.#Incorrect. The plant recovers.
}

::ENG1_U02_L02_Q25::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: At first, Layla wanted to keep the shortcut to the river secret. When she saw trash collecting near the bank, she invited friends to help clean it. Question: Which theme is strongest?{
=A. Protecting something may require sharing responsibility for it.#Correct. Layla shares the place so others can help care for it.
~B. Rivers should always stay secret.#Incorrect. Layla chooses to invite help.
~C. Friends always make rivers dirty.#Incorrect. Friends help clean.
~D. Shortcuts are the main problem in nature.#Incorrect. Trash and responsibility are the focus.
}
